1
00:00:04,974 --> 00:00:28,019
[SPEAKER_01]: So, today's sutta, well, two suttas actually, are Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama Gautama

2
00:00:32,532 --> 00:00:36,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Not picking up the sign of the mind, it is impossible that he will fulfill the right view.

3
00:00:36,435 --> 00:00:43,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Having not fulfilled the right view, it's impossible that he will fulfill the right composure.

4
00:00:55,283 --> 00:00:58,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Having not fulfilled the right composure, it's impossible that he will give up the fetters.

5
00:00:58,884 --> 00:01:02,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Having not given up the fetters, it's impossible that he will realize nibbāna.

6
00:01:04,265 --> 00:01:16,087
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the converse is that it is possible that a bhikkhu who does not enjoy association and company, who is not fond of them, and is not devoted to enjoying them, would enjoy being alone in seclusion.

7
00:01:16,247 --> 00:01:19,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Enjoying being alone in seclusion, it's possible that he will pick up the sign of the mind.

8
00:01:20,568 --> 00:01:23,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Picking up the sign of the mind, it's possible that he would fulfill the right view.

9
00:01:24,736 --> 00:01:30,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Having fulfilled the right view, it's possible that he'll fulfill the right composure, and having fulfilled the right composure, it's possible that he will give up the fetters.

10
00:01:30,979 --> 00:01:33,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Having given up the fetters, it's possible that he will realize nibbāna.

11
00:01:36,661 --> 00:01:52,648
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh... And then the second sutta, because this was fairly short, eh... Mīgajāla says at Sāvatī, The Venerable Mīgajāla went to the Blessed One, and on arrival, having bowed to the Blessed One, sat down to one side and said,

12
00:01:54,763 --> 00:01:58,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Bhante, one who dwells alone, one who dwells alone, so it is said.

13
00:01:58,546 --> 00:02:01,688
[SPEAKER_01]: To what extent is one a lone dweller?

14
00:02:02,368 --> 00:02:08,872
[SPEAKER_01]: To what extent is one dweller with a second I, with a company, with a partner, with somebody or something else?

15
00:02:10,313 --> 00:02:19,519
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha replied, Mīgijāla, there are forms cognizable by the eye that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasant, dear in appearance, connected with sensuality and enticing.

16
00:02:20,835 --> 00:02:30,818
[SPEAKER_01]: If a monk delights in them, approves of them, stands holding to them, then for him, eh... as he delights in them, approves of them, and stands holding for them, delight arises.

17
00:02:31,799 --> 00:02:35,140
[SPEAKER_01]: When there is delight, there is passion, and when there is passion, there is bondage.

18
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:40,702
[SPEAKER_01]: A monk who is bound by the fetter of delight is called the one who dwells with a second.

19
00:02:42,702 --> 00:02:50,505
[SPEAKER_01]: And then goes... repeat the same for the sounds, odors, tastes, tangibles, mental phenomena, the dhammas.

20
00:02:52,470 --> 00:03:07,299
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if a monk dwelling in this way, even though he frequents forests and woodland, wilderness lodgings, remote abodes with little noise and little sound, secluded, hidden from human beings, suitable for seclusion, he is still called the one who dwells with a second.

21
00:03:08,439 --> 00:03:09,720
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is the reason for that?

22
00:03:10,741 --> 00:03:15,123
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because craving is his second, that has not been abandoned by him.

23
00:03:15,423 --> 00:03:19,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, he's called one who dwells with a second, or one who dwells with a partner.

24
00:03:21,947 --> 00:03:23,388
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the Buddha explains the opposite.

25
00:03:23,428 --> 00:03:44,906
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when there are forms cognizable by the eye, that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasant, dear in appearance, connected with sensuality and enticing, but the monk does not delight in them, does not approve of them, does not stand holding to them, then for him, as he does not delight in them, doesn't approve them, does not stand holding to them, delight ceases.

26
00:03:44,966 --> 00:03:48,049
[SPEAKER_01]: And, obviously, the new delight would not be arising or being maintained.

27
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:51,344
[SPEAKER_01]: With delight not being, passion is not.

28
00:03:51,945 --> 00:03:54,389
[SPEAKER_01]: With passion not being, bondage is not.

29
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:58,856
[SPEAKER_01]: A monk who is disengaged from the fetter of delight is called Meghajāla, one who dwells alone.

30
00:03:59,477 --> 00:04:03,062
[SPEAKER_01]: And it is the same for sounds, other states, tangibles, mental phenomena.

31
00:04:05,512 --> 00:04:10,154
[SPEAKER_01]: And it says that the light for that monk ceases.

32
00:04:10,594 --> 00:04:26,639
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a monk dwelling in this way, Meghajāla, even though he dwells in the neighborhood of a village, crowded with monks, nuns, male lay followers, female lay followers, kings, royal ministers, ascetics of other sects and their followers, he's still called the one who dwells alone.

33
00:04:28,039 --> 00:04:29,120
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the reason for that?

34
00:04:30,071 --> 00:04:36,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Because craving is his second, and that craving has been abandoned by him, therefore, he's called the one who dwells alone.

35
00:04:37,734 --> 00:04:45,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you just go back, and now, overall, obviously, the picture that this paints is that, yes, one needs to be physically secluded.

36
00:04:46,736 --> 00:04:54,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Certainly, in the beginning, needs to frequent the secluded lodgings and so on, as the, you know, many suttas often talk about.

37
00:04:55,138 --> 00:05:08,842
[SPEAKER_01]: But, obviously, fundamentally, that, in and of itself, will not do the work of actually uprooting the companion, the partner, the second, the root of all the problems.

38
00:05:09,562 --> 00:05:10,923
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like...

39
00:05:12,503 --> 00:05:32,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Frequently secluded dwellings, learning how to delight in seclusion, learning how to how to find that peace in seclusion, it's absolutely a necessary step, so that then one can really look closer towards things that Mīgajala Sutta describes.

40
00:05:32,548 --> 00:05:38,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So, towards one's own delight, towards sight, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, thoughts,

41
00:05:39,815 --> 00:05:46,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether one is, as it says here, whether one approves of them, stands holding to them.

42
00:05:47,117 --> 00:05:54,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the attitude that one inadvertently cultivates, automatically cultivates, when agreeable things are experienced through the five senses and the mind.

43
00:05:55,880 --> 00:05:59,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the point here in this sutta, is to recognize that...

44
00:05:59,681 --> 00:06:05,063
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are forms cognizable, that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasant, dear in appearance.

45
00:06:05,743 --> 00:06:06,843
[SPEAKER_01]: They're sight, sound, smell, taste.

46
00:06:07,184 --> 00:06:08,184
[SPEAKER_01]: They're mental phenomena.

47
00:06:09,097 --> 00:06:15,486
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are thoughts that are agreeable, pleasant, and that's not the problem.

48
00:06:16,848 --> 00:06:21,975
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they're connected with sensuality, these thoughts, and they are enticing in and of themselves.

49
00:06:23,156 --> 00:06:23,377
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

50
00:06:24,793 --> 00:06:25,833
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just how they arise.

51
00:06:25,873 --> 00:06:26,954
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how they present themselves.

52
00:06:27,474 --> 00:06:33,836
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something that a monk or anybody wants to practice can change or forcefully undo or get rid of.

53
00:06:33,996 --> 00:06:41,219
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I keep saying to people, like, you have to stop, like, automatically trying to get rid of the things that arise pressuring you.

54
00:06:42,080 --> 00:06:53,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that, automatically accepting that attitude, trying to get rid of it, you are obscuring the more subtle attitude, which is, well, you delighting, approving, holding to it, basically being involved with it.

55
00:06:54,757 --> 00:07:10,747
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could try and destroy the sights or destroy the eyes in a form of, like, a desperate denial, because you can't help but do that through, first and foremost, being affected by delighting them, by approving of them, by holding on to them.

56
00:07:11,948 --> 00:07:19,073
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as the Sutta describes, the monk needs to stop that attitude of approval, welcoming, tolerating.

57
00:07:19,729 --> 00:07:26,431
[SPEAKER_01]: But not go too far out and then try to, like, prevent those things from mentally arising when they arise on their own.

58
00:07:27,468 --> 00:07:29,490
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you can try controlling it.

59
00:07:29,551 --> 00:07:34,856
[SPEAKER_01]: And, of course, in the beginning, yes, of course, you should withdraw yourself from the bombardment of your senses.

60
00:07:35,577 --> 00:07:48,751
[SPEAKER_01]: But not because that will, in and of itself, do the work, but because that can bring you closer to recognize where your delight, where you're holding on to those things, or your gratuitous resistance, if those sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches are unpleasant.

61
00:07:49,071 --> 00:07:49,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Your aversion.

62
00:07:51,123 --> 00:07:56,985
[SPEAKER_01]: So, either way you're maintaining the craving, either way you're dwelling with the second and you're not truly alone.

63
00:07:58,205 --> 00:08:04,408
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this kind of... these two suttas mirror that other sutta from Majjhima Nikaya where the Buddha describes the...

64
00:08:04,808 --> 00:08:08,889
[SPEAKER_01]: I often use that simile of the two wet... the two sticks... two wet sticks in the water.

65
00:08:09,709 --> 00:08:17,372
[SPEAKER_01]: They need to be physically taken out of the water first, but then they need to kind of... you need to wait for a while for them to dry.

66
00:08:19,075 --> 00:08:23,418
[SPEAKER_01]: On the shore, because otherwise, like, you still won't light the fire, because they're still wet.

67
00:08:24,079 --> 00:08:26,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although you have taken them from the water, they're wet.

68
00:08:27,241 --> 00:08:30,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if you start rubbing them together, there will be no sparks coming out.

69
00:08:31,063 --> 00:08:36,867
[SPEAKER_01]: But only after a while, when they have sort of dried out sufficiently, one can light the fire, as the sutra says.

70
00:08:36,987 --> 00:08:45,633
[SPEAKER_01]: And it refers to as long as one doesn't live withdrawn with the body, withdrawn from sensuality, obviously one's minds and thoughts will be soaking wet.

71
00:08:47,168 --> 00:09:02,751
[SPEAKER_01]: If one withdraws physically from the sensuality, yes, that's a good first step, but, again, one's minds and thoughts and attitudes and welcoming and approving and delighting and relishing and hoping and looking forward to, it still keeps the mind wet.

72
00:09:02,991 --> 00:09:10,193
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those attitudes need to be abandoned for the thoughts to truly dry from the entire kind of stretch of the sensual domain.

73
00:09:11,228 --> 00:09:19,793
[SPEAKER_01]: When the thoughts have been dried out, that's when one is capable of lighting the... developing the inside.

74
00:09:20,394 --> 00:09:24,536
[SPEAKER_01]: And this principle holds true, like, from Sotāpatti to Arahantship.

75
00:09:24,696 --> 00:09:25,657
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same inside.

76
00:09:26,737 --> 00:09:29,719
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's like... Like, we'll see.

77
00:09:29,759 --> 00:09:38,344
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I have a list of questions and contemplations in regard to seclusion in particular, but it's good to see, like, in this... the first sutta, Anguttara 668,

78
00:09:40,415 --> 00:09:57,905
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, we mentioned this sutta in the past number of times, and I think often... yeah, it's used kind of as one of the... an alternative description, certainly something that adds to the context of what is necessary to develop the right view.

79
00:09:59,466 --> 00:10:06,850
[SPEAKER_01]: I think often people take that sutta that talks about the two factors, which is the yoniso, manasikāra and utterance of another,

80
00:10:07,827 --> 00:10:27,368
[SPEAKER_01]: I think sometimes people take it, I think probably, partially, due to the certainly commentarial and traditional interpretation, they take it kind of as a matter of like a... it's like a static facts, like, okay, right, so I just need to hear the right thing at the right time and magically the insight will occur, regardless of, you know, where I'm at at that moment.

81
00:10:29,244 --> 00:10:46,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, obviously, not just it contradicts the whole notion of gradual training described in vast majority of the suttas, but even even the suttas where the Buddha did quickly, you know, make people sotāpānas, explain dhamma at the right time, to the right extent, even that had to follow the certain order.

82
00:10:46,770 --> 00:10:55,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Even that, you know, he would elevate their mind by talking about heavens and then, like, bring them down by talking about the perils of sensuality and make them renounce sensuality there and then.

83
00:10:56,325 --> 00:10:58,188
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he says, oh, he would know their mind.

84
00:10:58,468 --> 00:10:59,650
[SPEAKER_01]: He would know the mind is ready.

85
00:11:00,071 --> 00:11:01,173
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he would teach them the dhamma.

86
00:11:02,215 --> 00:11:07,618
[SPEAKER_01]: So, obviously, the Buddha was known as the foremost in formulating the dhamma and teaching the others.

87
00:11:07,698 --> 00:11:10,079
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he obviously possessed the skill to see the mind of the others.

88
00:11:10,699 --> 00:11:14,381
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the sutra is kind of extra interesting, so to speak.

89
00:11:14,441 --> 00:11:15,301
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because, like...

90
00:11:15,401 --> 00:11:18,223
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do you do when you don't have the Buddha to teach you?

91
00:11:18,743 --> 00:11:20,224
[SPEAKER_01]: What did monks did back then?

92
00:11:20,564 --> 00:11:22,225
[SPEAKER_01]: The ones that didn't meet the Buddha directly.

93
00:11:22,705 --> 00:11:23,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it tells you what they did.

94
00:11:24,386 --> 00:11:26,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, other sutras of the gradual training tell you as well.

95
00:11:27,727 --> 00:11:33,232
[SPEAKER_01]: But in this case, you could see, like, so, a person starts by not seeing their own mind whatsoever.

96
00:11:34,433 --> 00:11:47,765
[SPEAKER_01]: If that person's lucky enough, the Buddha will see the mind for them, and it will tell it the right things, and make them abandon the right fat... right obstacles, obstructions, not entirely the fetters, that... that requires their own work later, and then they will get the right view.

97
00:11:48,405 --> 00:11:51,288
[SPEAKER_01]: But if there's no Buddha now, like, what do you need to do?

98
00:11:53,672 --> 00:12:07,767
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have the utterance of another and say you are not looking for faults in the teaching, you are willing to consider anything that you hear as dhamma and really get to the bottom of understanding it and the meaning behind it, not just go by the tradition, by the hearsay and so on,

99
00:12:08,668 --> 00:12:11,331
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the other requirement?

100
00:12:11,792 --> 00:12:24,085
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the other requirements if you don't take that to mean... the two factors to mean, just as I said, like these static facts of just hearing the right thing at the right time and have a special experience that equates to sotāpati?

101
00:12:24,565 --> 00:12:25,707
[SPEAKER_01]: And this sutta describes that.

102
00:12:26,087 --> 00:12:28,429
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's impossible to fulfill the right view.

103
00:12:29,030 --> 00:12:32,494
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible if you are not able to see the signs of your mind.

104
00:12:32,554 --> 00:12:40,522
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're not able to see your mind for what it is, correctly, in its characteristics, its features, it's impossible for you to develop the right view.

105
00:12:42,704 --> 00:12:50,772
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be impossible for you to see the mind correctly, to see the feature, to see the nimitta, the signs of it, if you are not enjoying being alone in seclusion.

106
00:12:53,063 --> 00:13:02,151
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you won't enjoy being alone in seclusion, if you're fond of company, association, entanglement, interactions with people.

107
00:13:02,991 --> 00:13:06,474
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the point is, like, obviously, this sutta is already addressed to bhikkhus.

108
00:13:06,654 --> 00:13:12,039
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's already addressed to people that would have left the household life.

109
00:13:12,679 --> 00:13:15,702
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already addressed to people that would have been celibate.

110
00:13:16,443 --> 00:13:21,587
[SPEAKER_01]: They would not have been interacting in the, you know, the villagers' ways, as the suttas describe.

111
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:42,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, they, now, having given up the coarse, obviously, obstacles, obstructions, now they also have to start giving up that enjoyment in association, even if it's a wholesome association and wholesome company, not necessarily, like, really, really coarse or depraved and so on.

112
00:13:43,167 --> 00:13:51,350
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, simply depending on the company needs to now be undermined, needs to be understood and needs to be replaced by actually preferring seclusion.

113
00:13:52,544 --> 00:13:59,367
[SPEAKER_01]: So that you can get to see the signs of the mind, so that you can actually get that right view.

114
00:14:00,047 --> 00:14:10,831
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this paired with the suttas that describe the gradual training, kind of fits together and shows you, okay, this is how people would have trained.

115
00:14:11,901 --> 00:14:33,468
[SPEAKER_01]: People that, as I said, they were not guided directly by an Arahant or by the Buddha, you know, heard some instruction here and there, out of faith, decided to go in that direction, and sometimes even maybe didn't even have, like, not even hundredth of what we have today in terms of the texts and instructions, yet it was enough for them to develop the right view and to become Arahants.

116
00:14:34,648 --> 00:14:36,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, obviously, they were doing the work.

117
00:14:36,168 --> 00:14:41,310
[SPEAKER_01]: The work being withdrawal from the coarse impurities

118
00:14:42,962 --> 00:14:51,126
[SPEAKER_01]: And even in a household life, again, you could do the same, follow the same steps and what these suttas describe.

119
00:14:51,686 --> 00:14:55,388
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't necessarily need to become a monk for it, but you need to meet the requirements.

120
00:14:55,508 --> 00:14:56,968
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what are the requirements of the bhikkhus?

121
00:14:56,988 --> 00:14:57,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we all know that.

122
00:14:58,649 --> 00:14:59,770
[SPEAKER_01]: You meet those requirements.

123
00:14:59,890 --> 00:15:06,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, okay, your household life might be crowded and dusty, as the suttas would say, but within that,

124
00:15:07,955 --> 00:15:14,798
[SPEAKER_01]: You could work or you could certainly start becoming less and less dependent on association.

125
00:15:14,818 --> 00:15:22,741
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you do have a choice to associate yourself or not, do you choose to to stay alone or do you choose to follow the desire for company?

126
00:15:23,281 --> 00:15:36,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, company will be... you'll be pushed into the association, you will have to go through stuff, work, people, family, but the times when you don't have to do it, do you choose it still?

127
00:15:38,803 --> 00:15:40,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Or actually, do you start choosing seclusion?

128
00:15:41,384 --> 00:15:45,967
[SPEAKER_01]: And do you start working towards learning how to enjoy seclusion?

129
00:15:45,987 --> 00:15:48,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Because nobody enjoys seclusion to begin with.

130
00:15:48,770 --> 00:15:59,476
[SPEAKER_01]: And if people enjoy seclusion, there's puthujjana, it might be, you know, maybe because for one reason or the other they did not become that dependent on the company, but also it might be because they're averse to company.

131
00:15:59,896 --> 00:16:02,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not going to be a wholesome reasons for a seclusion.

132
00:16:02,457 --> 00:16:06,039
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to... you need to actually start re-evaluating those.

133
00:16:06,119 --> 00:16:12,543
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, as I said, if they're wholesome, great, but more often than not, it's going to be something other at play.

134
00:16:13,818 --> 00:16:20,339
[SPEAKER_01]: So, most people will have to work towards learning how to correctly, for the right reasons, enjoy seclusion.

135
00:16:20,439 --> 00:16:22,700
[SPEAKER_01]: How not to distract themselves from it.

136
00:16:23,300 --> 00:16:32,202
[SPEAKER_01]: How not to, kind of, sort of, like, use it to mask, actually, where the real work is.

137
00:16:33,482 --> 00:16:35,303
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the point to recognize here.

138
00:16:35,323 --> 00:16:35,643
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like,

139
00:16:36,748 --> 00:16:37,989
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not going to enjoy it immediately.

140
00:16:38,289 --> 00:16:39,390
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to work towards it.

141
00:16:40,030 --> 00:16:45,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can start working towards it, is when you stop choosing company, when you are presented with choice.

142
00:16:45,513 --> 00:16:47,214
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have an option to stay alone, to stay alone.

143
00:16:47,534 --> 00:16:50,216
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have longer options to be withdrawn from people, take them.

144
00:16:50,636 --> 00:16:57,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Just to practice this when you can, because otherwise, in a household setting, most of the time, it's going to be crowds and others and duties.

145
00:16:58,540 --> 00:17:06,085
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's still possible, but the fact that the household environment is different than the monk's environment,

146
00:17:06,605 --> 00:17:07,825
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just gonna make it harder.

147
00:17:07,846 --> 00:17:15,128
[SPEAKER_01]: It does not make it, like, justifiable to think or invent a different way to get to the same thing, because there isn't a different way.

148
00:17:15,529 --> 00:17:17,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Gradual training is the gradual training.

149
00:17:18,070 --> 00:17:27,474
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, whether a person can achieve periods of seclusion in their home or something, great.

150
00:17:27,574 --> 00:17:28,014
[SPEAKER_01]: If not,

151
00:17:29,021 --> 00:17:33,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe go to a monastery or go somewhere on your own when that's possible.

152
00:17:34,708 --> 00:17:39,654
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, use it pretty much to start reflecting in the right direction.

153
00:17:39,674 --> 00:17:40,475
[SPEAKER_01]: We're going to go through that.

154
00:17:40,515 --> 00:17:43,259
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a whole list of, like, reflections and things and so on.

155
00:17:44,360 --> 00:17:44,881
[SPEAKER_01]: But...

156
00:17:48,562 --> 00:18:04,091
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, I think it's kind of important to recognize that the question is not just about withdrawing yourself physically from people, from the association, and to feel like, okay, now my work is done, when is the Sotāpati coming my way?

157
00:18:05,531 --> 00:18:07,833
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, it comes when you develop it.

158
00:18:09,414 --> 00:18:13,776
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and also, yeah, another thing to mention before we go into question, it's like,

159
00:18:16,406 --> 00:18:17,287
[SPEAKER_01]: When it says seclusion...

160
00:18:17,307 --> 00:18:18,928
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, say you begin with the seclusion.

161
00:18:18,948 --> 00:18:21,250
[SPEAKER_01]: You begin by withdrawing yourself from the company.

162
00:18:21,530 --> 00:18:30,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily, you know, maybe you're not on the level of, like, still discerning all the attitudes of delighting towards the prospects of saṃsāra-samādhi-sthārtha, what Mīgajāla Sūtra talks about.

163
00:18:31,638 --> 00:18:37,963
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're just working on physically being withdrawn and then living with yourself for that period of time.

164
00:18:39,064 --> 00:18:40,204
[SPEAKER_01]: That's important to recognize.

165
00:18:40,365 --> 00:18:44,228
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you're withdrawn into that seclusion,

166
00:18:46,358 --> 00:18:56,042
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of easy to fall into a trap of then doing the practice for the sake of basically obscuring the discomfort of seclusion.

167
00:18:56,822 --> 00:19:08,388
[SPEAKER_01]: So, finding things to do, reading the suttas, studying or, you know, meditating or doing techniques often become a thing that you do so that you don't have to be alone with yourself.

168
00:19:09,208 --> 00:19:11,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, more precisely, you don't have to be alone with your mind.

169
00:19:13,388 --> 00:19:15,690
[SPEAKER_01]: And that basically means you're not in seclusion.

170
00:19:16,371 --> 00:19:17,311
[SPEAKER_01]: You're distracting yourself.

171
00:19:17,752 --> 00:19:24,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're not distracting yourself with extremely unwholesome stuff that you would have been doing in the world, but nevertheless, you're doing it for the same reasons.

172
00:19:26,063 --> 00:19:31,725
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why we have to recognize that when you're in seclusion, when you want to develop that seclusion, yeah, of course, it's not going to be perfect, you know.

173
00:19:32,205 --> 00:19:47,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, if you need to read something, read something, but you need to recognize that all of that has to lead to the point when you can abide in seclusion, not have or need anything to do, and be fine with yourself, and not lose the sight of your mind, the signs of the mind.

174
00:19:48,769 --> 00:19:51,290
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if suddenly you have nothing to do, you'll be absolutely fine.

175
00:19:52,182 --> 00:19:56,144
[SPEAKER_01]: There would be no, like, pressure building up from that animal that's forcing you.

176
00:19:56,325 --> 00:20:00,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, you gotta find some occupation, because otherwise this is getting unbearable.

177
00:20:00,327 --> 00:20:03,869
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm drowning in myself, pretty much.

178
00:20:03,989 --> 00:20:12,875
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is important, because, again, especially, like, you know, if you're still unsure about what the actual practice is, and maybe if you're not convinced,

179
00:20:14,150 --> 00:20:27,073
[SPEAKER_01]: By our arguments, when it comes to meditation or whatever, and if you still find meditation helpful and so on, it's going to be very easy to fall into meditating in seclusion, so that you are not alone with yourself.

180
00:20:28,173 --> 00:20:29,653
[SPEAKER_01]: And I've seen that happen to many people.

181
00:20:29,693 --> 00:20:38,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, one acquaintance of ours, a monk, he disrobed eventually, and he would... he used to go and stay in this really remote kutis in Sri Lanka.

182
00:20:39,900 --> 00:20:40,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, super remote.

183
00:20:40,682 --> 00:20:46,795
[SPEAKER_01]: You just go pindapad in the morning to the village, you don't even have to talk to anybody, and then you go back, and that's it.

184
00:20:47,879 --> 00:20:52,980
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no electricity, no... nowhere to even go for, like, nature hikes.

185
00:20:53,040 --> 00:20:55,141
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just absolute wilderness, hot, unpleasant.

186
00:20:55,181 --> 00:20:57,721
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just be sitting in your kuti till the next day.

187
00:20:57,821 --> 00:20:58,841
[SPEAKER_01]: No work, no duties.

188
00:20:58,861 --> 00:21:02,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Villagers would do anything that kuti needs, like, kuti needs repairs.

189
00:21:02,802 --> 00:21:10,304
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he would go there on these, like, you know, couple of weeks or longer, and always have, like, an expiry date, so to speak.

190
00:21:10,344 --> 00:21:15,825
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, in 10 days would always set something up that would require him to go, I don't know,

191
00:21:16,105 --> 00:21:19,446
[SPEAKER_01]: ...to the town, or to this place, or to that place, and then he would come back.

192
00:21:20,486 --> 00:21:32,668
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was doing that, and in the meantime, while he was there, he would go up in the path, do the chores, and then spend the entire afternoon watching the nostrils, doing the ānāpānasati, or sensations, or whatever technique he got into.

193
00:21:34,169 --> 00:21:41,390
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the time comes when he can go, and then he would feel, okay, now I can go, I did my work, and then he would go back and repeat that.

194
00:21:41,790 --> 00:21:44,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, on one occasion, one of his plans fell through,

195
00:21:45,659 --> 00:21:49,561
[SPEAKER_01]: And the person was supposed to pick him up, I don't know, Friday or something, and he didn't.

196
00:21:50,721 --> 00:21:52,202
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they said next Friday.

197
00:21:53,123 --> 00:21:58,825
[SPEAKER_01]: And by that point, he he was actually quite, you know, built up this tension, he couldn't sit anymore.

198
00:21:59,426 --> 00:22:06,149
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just the thought of having to spend another day meditating, was just filled him with anxiety.

199
00:22:06,769 --> 00:22:11,171
[SPEAKER_01]: So, obviously, the natural kind of conclusion is, well, I don't have to meditate.

200
00:22:11,251 --> 00:22:13,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, now that filled him with even greater anxiety.

201
00:22:13,712 --> 00:22:14,613
[SPEAKER_01]: What will I do?

202
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:34,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I sat for, like, ten days now, six hours a day every afternoon, till the evening, then I go to sleep, then pin the pad, then I sit six days, then six hours, then I go to sleep, then get up, pin the pad, do the chores, sit, and... and just got tired of that routine, couldn't do it anymore, and it's like, well, I don't have to sit, and now the panic really set in.

203
00:22:34,446 --> 00:22:37,267
[SPEAKER_01]: What will I do for the remaining of my time?

204
00:22:38,710 --> 00:22:42,151
[SPEAKER_01]: And he panicked and then on the next day he basically came to Hillside to visit us.

205
00:22:42,531 --> 00:22:49,873
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was quite quite distraught and wanted to talk and, you know, because I think part of him was shocked.

206
00:22:50,393 --> 00:22:52,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he thought he was doing the right practices.

207
00:22:52,714 --> 00:22:55,675
[SPEAKER_01]: He was doing all these techniques that all these great Ajahns teach and everything else.

208
00:22:56,475 --> 00:22:56,935
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like...

209
00:22:58,156 --> 00:23:02,297
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you still can't be with yourself, what use is any of that to you?

210
00:23:03,273 --> 00:23:22,195
[SPEAKER_01]: If, like, if you're not able to do it, or if there's, say, sounds, or, like, I don't know, you end up in a hospital or something, and you can't do your... your... your calming practices, your management techniques, what use is that to you, when your mind will go back to its own wilderness, and able to pressure and threaten you, and overwhelm you?

211
00:23:23,190 --> 00:23:24,571
[SPEAKER_01]: To the point that you run in panic.

212
00:23:24,751 --> 00:23:28,493
[SPEAKER_01]: For any help, any advice you can get, even from people that you were usually disagreeing with.

213
00:23:28,593 --> 00:23:30,414
[SPEAKER_01]: I... me.

214
00:23:30,794 --> 00:23:34,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyway, he was... he was asking questions, listening and stuff, and I explained this to him.

215
00:23:34,296 --> 00:23:44,922
[SPEAKER_01]: And you could see, like, that he understands that what I was explaining is something completely opposite of everything that he has been doing in the name of practice up to that point.

216
00:23:45,978 --> 00:24:01,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything that was bound with the sense of duty, that was bound with the meditation, that was bound with technique, that was bound with this expectation that results will come on their own, when I diligently just fulfill this mindless practice, which also conveniently keeps me occupied, so I don't have to be with myself.

217
00:24:03,285 --> 00:24:06,167
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why you have to recognize, okay, this was like a bit of an extreme example.

218
00:24:06,187 --> 00:24:11,932
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, he was following all these other things that we clearly, you know, and repeatedly describe as wrong views.

219
00:24:12,492 --> 00:24:21,479
[SPEAKER_01]: But even if you don't go to that extreme, you might still be doing the right stuff, you know, listening to Hillside or doing the Hillside way for the same reason.

220
00:24:22,728 --> 00:24:27,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, in the beginning you have to learn, you have to apply, you have to, like, re-evaluate and think and so on.

221
00:24:28,452 --> 00:24:37,819
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point comes when you recognize, you're in seclusion, and you will recognize, am I doing these things now because I really don't know what am I supposed to do?

222
00:24:38,379 --> 00:24:43,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Or it's because I have this urgent pressure that needs something, mine needs something to be occupied with?

223
00:24:44,706 --> 00:24:46,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have to be careful to recognize it.

224
00:24:46,548 --> 00:24:54,138
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you begin to truly find comfort and delight in pure seclusion of, like, non-activity, non-distraction.

225
00:24:55,685 --> 00:25:04,593
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what outlines the mind and cittanimitta sufficiently, thoroughly, and so on, for the fulfillment of the right view and everything else that follows.

226
00:25:05,253 --> 00:25:16,604
[SPEAKER_01]: So, only then, you can go to Migjala Sutta and recognize these subtler attitudes of non-delighting in that which has a reason, with the delightful thing that has a reason.

227
00:25:17,264 --> 00:25:24,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Not craving, not welcoming, not accepting the thing that's there on its own and being able to see that space between.

228
00:25:25,926 --> 00:25:33,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Because before that, it's like something arises, I must either act towards it or try and deny it, destroy it, choke it out, asphyxiate it.

229
00:25:33,567 --> 00:25:44,469
[SPEAKER_01]: And none of those actions or reactions, whether it's a lust or aversion, both or rather both of those directions, do not reveal and further obscure the mind, the animal.

230
00:25:45,304 --> 00:25:46,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Wear your attitude.

231
00:25:46,665 --> 00:25:51,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, what the Sutta describes like that, he doesn't welcome, he doesn't delight.

232
00:25:51,650 --> 00:25:55,073
[SPEAKER_01]: That describes an attitude, your attitude, towards your own mind.

233
00:25:57,915 --> 00:26:04,281
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something that's not seen for as long as you're engaged positively or negatively with sight, sounds, smell, taste, touches and thoughts.

234
00:26:05,201 --> 00:26:12,348
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the whole point of being first withdrawn, not distracting yourself, so that the mind can, like, be seen, can reveal itself.

235
00:26:13,251 --> 00:26:19,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you could basically start learning, as I said in other talks, how to talk to it and so on.

236
00:26:20,994 --> 00:26:26,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I did write down a list of, say, somebody who is trying to develop seclusion.

237
00:26:26,816 --> 00:26:31,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, layperson or monk, it doesn't really matter.

238
00:26:31,377 --> 00:26:33,278
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to wait until...

239
00:26:34,378 --> 00:26:40,000
[SPEAKER_01]: I can start looking at my mind, or looking for my mind, or trying to discern my mind, only when I can be alone.

240
00:26:40,120 --> 00:26:42,141
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, you start doing that all the time.

241
00:26:43,128 --> 00:26:52,450
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that the periods, the actual periods of physical seclusion will be necessary to discern those signs correctly.

242
00:26:52,610 --> 00:27:00,752
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, if you have not already been working in that direction, no amount of signs will magically reveal themselves on account of you just being alone, suddenly.

243
00:27:01,933 --> 00:27:11,735
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to start working in that direction, the point is to start pretty much undermining the attitudes that are keeping you bound.

244
00:27:12,693 --> 00:27:17,916
[SPEAKER_01]: With association, with the need for association and company, finding safety in those things.

245
00:27:18,217 --> 00:27:21,399
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like, again, no magic pill, no magic technique.

246
00:27:21,579 --> 00:27:36,168
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to just start drilling, questioning, contemplation and reflection until those attitudes are sufficiently undermined and redirected and emphasized elsewhere towards seclusion and withdrawal from those very things that obstruct the right view.

247
00:27:37,209 --> 00:27:38,490
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like...

248
00:27:38,570 --> 00:27:39,891
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I was asking...

249
00:27:40,011 --> 00:27:41,392
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you seek company,

250
00:27:42,667 --> 00:27:43,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, you're fine.

251
00:27:43,507 --> 00:27:44,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, you can't help it.

252
00:27:44,507 --> 00:27:45,788
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't help but want company.

253
00:27:46,188 --> 00:27:53,529
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you do so, instead of just taking it at face value and seeking company, start asking yourself certain questions.

254
00:27:53,569 --> 00:27:58,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Start revealing more about that attitude, that kind of pressuring mood behind.

255
00:27:59,331 --> 00:28:03,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you're seeking company, why are you doing so?

256
00:28:03,232 --> 00:28:04,432
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you trying to get rid of?

257
00:28:06,298 --> 00:28:10,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, you're gonna get a relief, some form of relief, by seeking company.

258
00:28:10,480 --> 00:28:11,500
[SPEAKER_01]: But relief from what?

259
00:28:12,321 --> 00:28:14,442
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it that you need to be relieved from?

260
00:28:17,383 --> 00:28:18,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, you'll see the answer to all this.

261
00:28:18,964 --> 00:28:19,724
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your own mind.

262
00:28:20,224 --> 00:28:23,005
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, again, seeking company obscures the mind.

263
00:28:23,165 --> 00:28:24,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Obscure the mind, no sotāpati.

264
00:28:26,247 --> 00:28:33,710
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop seeking company, or rather, you're seeking company because the pressure of your own mind is something you want to be relieved from.

265
00:28:35,448 --> 00:28:36,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the animal is unpleasant.

266
00:28:38,669 --> 00:28:40,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Allowing it to be closer to me, it's even more unpleasant.

267
00:28:41,009 --> 00:28:46,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let me just distract myself and postpone and procrastinate and, you know, I'll deal with it later.

268
00:28:48,091 --> 00:28:52,772
[SPEAKER_01]: And unless you start questioning that attitude, it's never gonna disappear magically on its own.

269
00:28:54,092 --> 00:29:01,934
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to start bringing it up whenever you desire a company, want to choose the association and distraction through the means of others,

270
00:29:02,941 --> 00:29:11,105
[SPEAKER_01]: You actually don't act out on that and start questioning and revealing what is the underlying thing that I'm trying to relieve myself from.

271
00:29:11,645 --> 00:29:12,465
[SPEAKER_01]: And why is it a problem?

272
00:29:13,386 --> 00:29:14,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it really that unpleasant?

273
00:29:16,207 --> 00:29:20,169
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, if it is, that's... again, that should be a reason for concern.

274
00:29:20,329 --> 00:29:21,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Means I'm unpleasant to myself.

275
00:29:22,563 --> 00:29:23,604
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't live with my own mind.

276
00:29:23,724 --> 00:29:24,264
[SPEAKER_01]: I need others.

277
00:29:24,944 --> 00:29:25,665
[SPEAKER_01]: I need association.

278
00:29:25,685 --> 00:29:26,245
[SPEAKER_01]: I need company.

279
00:29:26,265 --> 00:29:26,465
[SPEAKER_01]: I need...

280
00:29:26,925 --> 00:29:27,125
[SPEAKER_01]: I need...

281
00:29:27,426 --> 00:29:31,348
[SPEAKER_01]: I need connections, because otherwise, I cannot live with myself.

282
00:29:31,488 --> 00:29:35,470
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's... that should be the most concerning thing to everybody.

283
00:29:36,831 --> 00:29:42,914
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, say, on the other hand, there is no company, there is no option for company.

284
00:29:42,934 --> 00:29:45,215
[SPEAKER_01]: You are... just happen to be alone, nothing to do.

285
00:29:46,917 --> 00:29:49,698
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the underlying tension, like, beneath that?

286
00:29:50,618 --> 00:29:53,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there like a building up of restlessness?

287
00:29:54,760 --> 00:29:56,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there like a discomfort that's pushing you?

288
00:29:56,861 --> 00:30:00,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I must do this, I must do that, I must occupy... Again, don't take it for granted.

289
00:30:02,143 --> 00:30:08,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Ask yourself, okay, so this is factually what I'm feeling, this is factually what mind is pushing me towards, but I don't need to act upon it.

290
00:30:09,145 --> 00:30:10,746
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to automatically distract myself.

291
00:30:11,326 --> 00:30:13,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how justifiable it seems.

292
00:30:13,267 --> 00:30:13,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me just

293
00:30:15,132 --> 00:30:22,896
[SPEAKER_01]: For just a short period of time, actually question this attitude, as opposed to how I always just do, accept it, and act out of it, and mask it.

294
00:30:23,797 --> 00:30:30,581
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is it that I'm trying to run away from, if I seek things to do, when there's nothing to do?

295
00:30:32,682 --> 00:30:33,802
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, let me not do it.

296
00:30:33,902 --> 00:30:34,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me just sit with this.

297
00:30:35,743 --> 00:30:36,564
[SPEAKER_01]: See how far it will go.

298
00:30:38,185 --> 00:30:41,447
[SPEAKER_01]: See what other things will mind start throwing at me as a result of it.

299
00:30:42,407 --> 00:30:43,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's the true meditation, really.

300
00:30:46,250 --> 00:31:08,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, obviously, you could say... say you're doing all of this now, then another line of reflection you can have is like... so, can I... can I remember, can I recognize the actual peacefulness or at least some aspect of when I was alone, not distracting myself with duties or actions and so on, not choosing company,

301
00:31:09,705 --> 00:31:12,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Overall, it doesn't feel right, doesn't feel pleasant.

302
00:31:12,769 --> 00:31:24,765
[SPEAKER_01]: I am pushed and pressured to... boredom is building up, but is there any aspect of that that I could recollect or, you know, kind of re-evaluate and actually recognize it as agreeable?

303
00:31:25,506 --> 00:31:34,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Overall, yes, it's not going to be agreeable, not in the beginning, but are there any aspects of it that I could factually say, oh, but no, that was actually agreeable, that I do prefer that aspect.

304
00:31:34,334 --> 00:31:39,038
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that, yeah, when you're alone, okay, well, you don't have to deal with others, in that sense.

305
00:31:39,638 --> 00:31:48,286
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to try as hard to not lose the context, because the simple non-engagement with others means maintenance of the context, even if you're not making the effort.

306
00:31:49,687 --> 00:31:50,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Are there any other?

307
00:31:51,214 --> 00:32:01,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Aspects... however, like, brief or fleeting, of the actual seclusion, set up rightly, that you could recall as pleasant and agreeable.

308
00:32:01,761 --> 00:32:03,643
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yes, recall them more often than not.

309
00:32:04,923 --> 00:32:07,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Try and... try and spread them... spread them around.

310
00:32:09,226 --> 00:32:11,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplate them, so they become even more pronounced.

311
00:32:12,789 --> 00:32:18,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of just, obviously, focusing on the negative pressure of the boredom and the restless mind that's trying to get you to... to act out.

312
00:32:21,688 --> 00:32:35,176
