674
01:02:52,560 --> 01:02:53,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't do it.

675
01:02:53,561 --> 01:02:54,641
[SPEAKER_01]: This passion falls out.

676
01:02:55,041 --> 01:02:55,622
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

677
01:02:55,642 --> 01:03:00,404
[SPEAKER_01]: They went to seclusion and fulfilled the goal of the Buddha's dispensation and became arahants.

678
01:03:00,464 --> 01:03:02,885
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

679
01:03:02,905 --> 01:03:11,169
[SPEAKER_01]: They actually stuck to it and worked their way through it in the correct manner, did not act out of things they shouldn't act out, protected things they should protect, and that's it.

680
01:03:12,090 --> 01:03:17,192
[SPEAKER_01]: All of everything else that Buddha talked about, all these symptoms and stuff, fell off, never to return.

681
01:03:21,796 --> 01:03:22,776
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite interesting.

682
01:03:23,416 --> 01:03:43,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe, over time, it seems in your teachings even, like, if you go and read meanings or maybe the earlier discussions, there seems to be that sort of emphasis, but then it seems like, at a certain point, you just cut that out and you said, like, maybe this exact problem that you're talking, that we're talking about now, sort of...

683
01:03:44,737 --> 01:03:49,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, in the beginning, you know, I've announced the question.

684
01:03:49,982 --> 01:04:07,236
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter where people were coming from, but after a while, and you do it for a few years, it's like, okay, I, you know, I have to be as efficient as possible, because I cannot be repeating myself 20 years down the line, just saying the same stuff, without any effect or benefit to a person, just basically just satisfying their curiosities.

685
01:04:08,789 --> 01:04:19,156
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you start to tailor, like, talk about things that matter and bring down, bring everything back down to where actually the difference can be made.

686
01:04:21,558 --> 01:04:26,541
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, whether people choose to follow it or not, that's fine, but I know that if they follow it, they will arrive at the result.

687
01:04:28,522 --> 01:04:36,908
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you know, if you just give them the answer, to whatever angle they came from, it does not necessarily guarantee they will make the actual effort that needs to be made, and so on.

688
01:04:38,702 --> 01:04:41,684
[SPEAKER_01]: So, initially, you do need answers, you do need to learn.

689
01:04:42,345 --> 01:04:50,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, Yanavera was very useful for me, although, yes, he was also just on the level of theoretical answers for as far as anybody comes to read it, you know.

690
01:04:50,772 --> 01:04:57,738
[SPEAKER_01]: He was doing the lifestyle of it, but that's not something you could have just emulated through reading his books.

691
01:04:57,998 --> 01:05:05,344
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where many people fall into the trap of just writing papers and quoting Yanavera and being convinced he's the most right person in the world, but they're not taught upon as themselves.

692
01:05:07,037 --> 01:05:12,862
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is necessary and useful, especially because everybody comes with lots of preconceived notions.

693
01:05:13,122 --> 01:05:18,567
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, the point is, there is a more direct way to deal with the preconceived notions.

694
01:05:19,776 --> 01:05:22,698
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start taming your mind, which is all, like, well documented in the suttas,

695
01:05:40,619 --> 01:05:49,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Start questioning your preconceived notions, your disagreements, your little preferences, and you will arrive at the obvious that you kind of know, which is why you were resisting what I'm saying from the start.

696
01:05:50,073 --> 01:05:51,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Not you specifically, but...

697
01:05:51,889 --> 01:05:54,110
[SPEAKER_01]: When people try to... well, this and that, you already know.

698
01:05:54,150 --> 01:06:03,416
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were authentic, transparent enough, you would know why you were resisting the gradual training that we describe, the way we describe, the way the suttas describe it.

699
01:06:03,516 --> 01:06:05,217
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you don't want it that way.

700
01:06:05,337 --> 01:06:09,559
[SPEAKER_01]: You want your preferences to be served, because this is too frightening, too scary, or whatever else.

701
01:06:10,340 --> 01:06:12,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just need some transparency.

702
01:06:12,121 --> 01:06:17,044
[SPEAKER_01]: And it would have saved me a lot of time, and I'm trying to save other people time, by telling them that's the point.

703
01:06:18,025 --> 01:06:22,408
[SPEAKER_01]: You question everything, including your own stances, and you will arrive at the transparency.

704
01:06:22,428 --> 01:06:34,615
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not going to feel pleasant, but it's certainly going to position you a lot better for actually then understanding what the essence of the Buddha's teaching was and the training.

705
01:06:37,357 --> 01:06:46,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I think that's really important to go back and sort of

706
01:06:46,719 --> 01:06:56,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep that emphasis in mind, especially because within, let's say, the phenomenological Buddhist circle, there's that sort of vibe to just, like, figure these things out.

707
01:06:56,161 --> 01:06:58,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can see things, get distracted.

708
01:06:58,662 --> 01:06:59,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

709
01:07:00,442 --> 01:07:02,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, it's not like, I must stop thinking about it.

710
01:07:02,343 --> 01:07:02,503
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

711
01:07:02,523 --> 01:07:11,865
[SPEAKER_01]: It's when you want to think about, instead of just taking it at face value, broaden the context and recognize the experience as a whole of wanting to think about it.

712
01:07:12,862 --> 01:07:14,083
[SPEAKER_01]: See the underlying pressure.

713
01:07:14,164 --> 01:07:15,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't just take it and go with it.

714
01:07:15,966 --> 01:07:16,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Include it.

715
01:07:16,646 --> 01:07:16,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

716
01:07:16,987 --> 01:07:17,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Include it.

717
01:07:17,908 --> 01:07:18,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

718
01:07:18,528 --> 01:07:18,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Include it.

719
01:07:19,730 --> 01:07:19,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

